a common challenge for improvement teams  is identifying what changes you can test  to improve a processor problem one great  method to help you break down ideas as a  tool known as a cause and effect diagram  you may have also heard of this tool  referred to as an Ishikawa diagram  attributed to its creator kuru Ishikawa  or a fishbone diagram because of the  resemblance to the bones of a fish to  create a cause and effect diagram right  the effect in a box on the right hand  side of the page so let's imagine we're  working on improving hand washing all  right then draw a horizontal line across  the page  decide on a few categories or causes for  the problem useful categories of causes  in a classic fish bone diagram include  people methods materials equipment and  environment then we can draw a diagonal  line to each of these and these are the  actually the the bones of the fish and  hence its name the process of  categorizing potential causes may be  very useful to help you to break down a  complex problem and focus in on it from  various perspectives so as you're  looking at each of the categories you  want to generate a list of causes in  this example people might include  clinical staff  right it also might include a  non-clinical staff I'll just put them as  just other staffers it might include the  methods how do you actually wash your  hands I'll say wash process it could be  the materials or using the right kind of  soap or is it dry out your hand so we'll  think about the soap it could be the  equipment like the dispenser it's an  easy dispenser to use does it give you  the right amount or maybe it's the  environment where is the dispenser  location is it near the process of care  identify the causes by you know asking  why with people that are closest to the  process until you've reached a useful  level of detail that is when the cause  is specific enough to be able to test a  change and to measure its effects  sometimes I like to describe this for  folks that have children as the  five-year-old method you know if you  ever had a kid they ask you why five  times constantly until they get to the  root of the problem and you're doing the  same thing here  you
 

